# GoKlay deployment configuration.
#
# Copy to .env and edit. Every value below is read once at process start, so
# changing one means restarting the API. Nothing here is a business rule:
# radius, fees, COD limits and commission are tunable at runtime by an admin
# and live in the config module, not in this file. See ADR 0004.
#
# ONLY these variables need to change when you deploy. Point them at your
# database, your Redis and your public hostname, build the Flutter apps against
# the same PUBLIC_BASE_URL, and the stack works.
#
# Commit .env.example. Never commit .env.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Environment
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# development | staging | production
# "production" turns on strict startup checks: a real signing key is required
# and PUBLIC_BASE_URL must be https. The process refuses to start otherwise,
# which is the point — a misconfigured production server should not come up.
APP_ENV=development

# Turns this deployment into a public demonstration. See docs/demo.md.
#
# It changes exactly two things: one-time codes are shown to whoever asks for
# one instead of being sent by SMS, and a payment against the manual gateway
# can be completed from the app. Everything else — rate limits, lockouts, code
# expiry, ownership checks — is unchanged.
#
# A demo is therefore a deployment where anyone can sign in as anyone. Put
# nothing in its database that would matter if it were public.
#
# It cannot be set in production: the process refuses to start. Set your own
# JWT_SIGNING_KEY even for a demo — see below for why.
DEMO_MODE=false

# debug | info | warn | error
LOG_LEVEL=info

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Network
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The address the API binds inside its container or host.
API_ADDR=:8080

# The URL clients actually call. Behind a load balancer or reverse proxy this
# differs from API_ADDR — it is the hostname the apps and browsers see.
#
# THIS IS THE ONE THE MOBILE APPS ARE BUILT AGAINST. The Flutter builds take it
# as --dart-define=GOKLAY_API_BASE_URL, so it ships inside the installed app and
# changing it later means a new build. Get it right before you release.
PUBLIC_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8080

# Web origins allowed to call the API, comma separated. Empty means none, which
# is correct for a mobile-only deployment. The admin panel is a web app, so it
# goes here once it has a hostname.
# CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://admin.goklay.com,https://merchant.goklay.com
CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# How long in-flight requests get to finish after SIGTERM.
SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT=10s

# How often the live order-tracking stream (GET /v1/track/{orderId}) polls
# order and dispatch for a change to send. A deploy-time trade-off between
# server load and how quickly a customer's screen updates — not an Appendix B
# business rule.
TRACKING_STREAM_INTERVAL=3s

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database — PostgreSQL with PostGIS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# PostGIS is required, not optional: merchant discovery (ALG-01) needs
# ST_DWithin over a GiST index and division containment (ALG-03) needs
# ST_Contains. A plain Postgres will fail on the first migration.
# See ADR 0003.
#
# Managed Postgres (RDS, Cloud SQL, Neon, Supabase): use sslmode=require.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://delivery:delivery@localhost:5432/delivery?sslmode=disable

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Redis — sessions, refresh tokens, rate limiting
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Refresh tokens are stored hashed with a TTL, so revocation and rotation are
# O(1) and expiry is automatic. See ADR 0005 and docs/build/phases/P04.md.
#
# With a password: redis://:yourpassword@host:6379/0
# Over TLS (Upstash, ElastiCache in-transit): rediss://...
REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379/0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Authentication
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Signs access tokens. REQUIRED in production, minimum 32 characters.
#
# This key is never written to the database. Anything in the database is
# readable by an admin, and an admin who can read the signing key can mint a
# token for any user — including another admin. Keep it in the environment or a
# secrets manager.
#
# Generate one with:  openssl rand -base64 48
#
# Rotating it invalidates every access token immediately. Refresh tokens live
# in Redis and survive, so clients recover on their next refresh rather than
# being logged out — that is what makes rotation something you can actually do.
JWT_SIGNING_KEY=

# The `iss` claim. Tokens issued by another environment are rejected rather
# than silently accepted, so a staging token cannot be replayed at production.
JWT_ISSUER=goklay

# Signs and verifies inbound payment gateway webhooks (P13). Same rule as the
# JWT key: never store this in the database, generate a real one per
# environment, at least 32 characters. Development gets a fixed placeholder so
# the stack runs with no secrets; production refuses to start without a real
# one.
PAYMENT_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Quick start
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   docker compose up -d postgres redis
#   cp .env.example .env
#   set -a && . ./.env && set +a
#   go run ./backend/cmd/migrate up      # create or update the schema
#   go run ./backend/cmd/migrate seed    # demo data; refused in production
#   go run ./backend/cmd/api
#
# `migrate up` is safe to run on every deploy: an up-to-date database applies
# nothing. `migrate status` shows what is applied, pending or dirty.
